  "slug": "arabidopsis-mediated-f99c",
  "summary": "New plant studies map regulators of ABA, JA and bud-outgrowth hormones to specific promoters, histone marks, splice events and miRNA targets.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is regulatory control of hormone biosynthesis, catabolism and sensitivity at defined genomic or transcript targets. ABA is the most frequent node: GhNAC029 represses GhNCED1 in cotton, RhWUS1 represses RhNCED3 in rose, methylation of the BraCYP707A3.A02 promoter blocks BraBPC2.A08 binding in Brassica, and SRPKII-SR45 control of HAB1 splicing shapes ABA sensitivity in Arabidopsis. ANAC030 promotes senescence through JA synthesis, and RSI1/FLD demethylates H3K4me2/3 at its own TSS to self-limit pathogen-induced expression. The miR159a-BrMYB12 module acts on raffinose-pathway genes for heat tolerance, and maize H2A.Z mapping provides chromatin context for tissue-specific expression. New is the resolution to specific promoters, epigenetic marks, splice events and miRNA targets.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
